  {
   "k": "yk5kt0",
   "en": "The Dutch East Indies formally came into existence in January 1st 1800 when the Dutch government seized the assets of Dutch East India Company (⟦0⟧ or the ⟦1⟧) which had declared bankruptcy. After a short French and British interregnums during the Napoleonic Wars, the colony was returned to the Dutch rule and promptly expanded colonial rule in the archipelago, finally finished after the defeat of Atjeh rebels in 1912 and the conquest of Vogelkop (Bird's Head Peninsula) in 1920.",
   "frags": 2
  },
  {
   "k": "3y31u2",
   "en": "Vereenigde Oostindische Compagnie"
  },
  {
   "k": "1l5i1xf",
   "en": "VOC"
  },
  {
   "k": "1oz1bbo",
   "en": "Politically, the colony experienced a small, but continuous, reform since the implementation of Ethical Policies (Ethische Politiek) in 1901, which has resulted in formation of several political parties such as Indische Partij (led by Ernest Douwes Dekker), Partai Nasional Indonesia (led by Sukarno), Partai Komunis Indonesia (led by Semaun), and Partai Indonesia Raya (led by Dokter Sutomo). But flare-ups from revolutionary elements in the 1926 Communist Rebellion has significantly pushed back the reforms through a political crackdown which has lasted until 1935. Thus, the Volksraad (People's Council) that has been formed as a quasi-parliament for a future independent Indies government has also had its power significantly reduced compared to the Governor-General's. Furthermore, along with the Indonesian communists, the leaders of the nascent Indonesian nationalist movement has also jailed in the crackdown, such as Sukarno to the island of Flores and Mohammad Hatta and Sutan Syahrir to Boven-Digoel (at Dutch New Guinea)."
  },
  {
   "k": "47okw6",
   "en": "Economically, the colony is at a deep crisis, having its economy plummeted by De Malaise (otherwise known as the Great Depression). Wages were being cut down and even the projects related to Ethical Policies were stopped to also helped bearing the economic burden from the ⟦0⟧' own Crisisjaren. Exports such as oil, rubber, and cash crops were also plummeted with European and American markets fizzled out because of the ongoing crisis, with a new player, ⟦1⟧ has gaining more and more shares of both import and export markets from the East Indies. The Directory of Economic Affairs under the leadership of George Charles Hart and his vice-director Hubertus Johannes van Mook has mitigated De Malaise's worst effects by giving land grants to the village-oriented plantations and public ownership of communications and finance facilities as welfare measures to keep the populace calm, to the dismay of Dutch industrialists.",
   "frags": 2
  },
  {
   "k": "rxz7u3",
   "en": "Netherlands"
  },
  {
   "k": "193rgu3",
   "en": "Japan"
  },
  {
   "k": "10d85ns",
   "en": "Reeling with the effects of the Malaise, inadequate military in both weaponry, manpower, and discipline, and also the reactionary colonial government maintaining its repressive power, the East Indies is at a crossroads in 1936."
  },
